{"id":"b0380e12-ee1e-47b5-bb66-68dceab224a2","object":"chat.completion","created":1785027898,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"import heapq\\n\\ndef solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    threshold = case['utility_threshold']\\n    \\n    # Build node map and children lists\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n['parent']\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\\n    \\n    present = {n['id'] for n in nodes}\\n    \\n    # Priority queue: descending depth, stable node id\\n    heap = []\\n    enqueued = set()\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        heapq.heappush(heap, (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n        enqueued.add(n['id'])\\n    \\n    visit_order = []\\n    \\n    while heap:\\n        neg_depth, node_id = heapq.heappop(heap)\\n        enqueued.discard(node_id)\\n        if node_id not in present:\\n            continue\\n        visit_order.append(node_id)\\n        node = node_map[node_id]\\n        if node['removable'] and node['utility'] <= threshold:\\n            # Check if leaf (no present children)\\n            is_leaf = all(child not in present for child in children[node_id])\\n            if is_leaf:\\n                present.remove(node_id)\\n                parent_id = node['parent']\\n                if parent_id is not None and parent_id in present:\\n                    # Remove child from parent's children list\\n                    children[parent_id].remove(node_id)\\n                    if parent_id not in enqueued:\\n                        heapq.heappush(heap, (-node_map[parent_id]['depth'], parent_id))\\n                        enqueued.add(parent_id)\\n    \\n    retained = sorted(present)\\n    return {'retained_node_ids': retained, 'visit_order': visit_order}\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write a solution for the task SE-PE-02. The description is a bit cryptic, but we have the public fixtures: nodes with parent relationship, removable flag, utility and depth. There is a utility_threshold. The objective: maintain a queue of nodes to process, in descending depth and stable node-ID order. Start with all nodes? Or some initial queue? The description: \"Initialize a queue in descending depth and stable node-ID order. Skip nodes already removed. Remove only a removable leaf whose utility is at most the threshold. After removal enqueue its retained parent for reconsideration. Return retained IDs and the complete visit order.\"\n\nSo the process seems to be a reduction (pruning) algorithm on a tree. We have nodes with parent pointers (null for root). The root is depth 0, not removable. We have other nodes. We need to repeatedly process a queue: we start with all nodes? But we need to process in order of descending depth, and stable node-ID order. So we can sort nodes by depth descending, then by id (string) ascending? \"stable node-ID order\" likely means alphabetical or numeric? Since IDs are like \"n1\", \"n2\", etc., presumably string comparison. We'll assume lexicographic order.\n\nWe need to simulate a queue: we process nodes in that order. For each node, if it's already removed (we track removed set), skip. Else if it is a leaf (no children? Or children that are not removed? Actually leaf means no children in the tree, but children could be removed? Probably leaf means no children that are still present? The description: \"Remove only a removable leaf whose utility is at most the threshold.\" So we need to decide if a node is a leaf based on current state? Initially, all nodes have their children based on parent relationships. After removal of children, a node may become a leaf. Also, removable nodes are those with removable=true. So we need to check if a node is leaf (no children that are not removed), and if removable and utility <= threshold.\n\nIf we remove a node, we then enqueue its retained parent (i.e., the parent if it is not removed) for reconsideration. The parent might have become a leaf after removal of that child.\n\nWe need to return retained_node_ids (the nodes that are not removed at the end) and visit_order (the order in which we visited nodes? \"the complete visit order\" - likely the order we popped from queue, including skipped? Or only those we actually processed? Probably the order we considered each node? The description: \"Return retained IDs and the complete visit order.\" So we need the list of node IDs in the order they were visited (popped from queue). The queue includes all nodes initially, but we process in descending depth, stable id. When we add parent after removal, we need to insert it into the queue appropriately to maintain the order? Or simply enqueue at the end? The description: \"After removal enqueue its retained parent for reconsideration.\" That suggests we add it to the queue for later processing. Since we process in descending depth, we might need to insert it in the correct position to maintain the queue order. But the algorithm description: \"Initialize a queue in descending depth and stable node-ID order. ... After removal enqueue its retained parent for reconsideration.\" It doesn't specify exactly how to re-insert. Typically in such algorithms, you would maintain a priority queue (e.g., heap) with key (depth descending, id). Or you can just append to a list and then sort later? But we need to simulate the queue dynamically.\n\nGiven the small size and deterministic nature, we can simulate as: start with a list of all node IDs sorted by depth descending, then by id. Then iterate through that list (as a queue). When we remove a node, we add its parent to a list of nodes to reconsider. After finishing the initial queue, we then process the reconsideration list, but we need to keep the ordering. Actually, the parent might have been processed already earlier because higher depth? But parent is at lower depth, so it would have been processed earlier if we processed all nodes in descending depth. So after removing a child, the parent was already visited. But the description says \"enqueue its retained parent for reconsideration\" meaning we should add it back to the queue to be processed again. So we need to re-add it, and then continue processing in the correct order. Since parent has lower depth, it would be placed after all nodes of same depth? Actually descending depth order: nodes with depth 2 first, then depth 1, then depth 0. The parent of a depth 2 node is depth 1, so parent would have been processed earlier if we process all depth 2 then all depth 1. But if we remove a depth 2 node, we want to reconsider its parent (depth 1) because it might become a leaf. But parent already processed. So we need to re-add it to the queue to be processed again. Since the queue order is descending depth, and the parent has higher depth than later nodes? Actually after removing depth 2, we still have other depth 2 nodes maybe. The parent is depth 1, which is lower depth, meaning it would be processed later in the queue if we maintained order (since depth 2 comes first). But we already processed depth 1 nodes earlier. So to maintain the order we need to insert the parent at the appropriate position among nodes of that depth? But after processing all nodes in initial order, we might need to repeatedly process the queue until no more removals. So we can implement a loop: maintain a queue (list) sorted by depth descending, id ascending. Process from front. When we remove a node, add its parent (if not removed) to a list of nodes to be re-inserted. After processing all nodes in current queue, we need to merge the new nodes into the queue in the proper order. But note that the parent might already be in the queue (if not yet processed?) Actually parent was processed earlier, but we are about to re-add it. So we need to ensure we don't have duplicates. Better to use a set of node IDs that are pending in the queue to avoid duplicates. And maintain a priority queue (heap) with keys. Python's heapq is min-heap, so we can push (-depth, id) for descending depth, and then pop. That will give correct order. We also need to keep track of visited nodes? The algorithm: \"Skip nodes already removed.\" So when we pop, if node is already removed, skip. Also, we need to ensure we don't process a node multiple times? But with re-adding parent, it could be added multiple times. We can use a visited set to avoid processing the same node more than once? But the algorithm says \"no_invalidated_revisit\" constraint: we should not revisit nodes that are invalidated? Actually \"no_invalidated_revisit\" means we should not revisit a node that has been invalidated? Not sure. But we should only process a node if it is still present in the tree. If we pop and it's already removed, skip. Also if it's already been processed and not removed? But after removal of child, the parent might become a leaf, so we need to process it again. So we need to allow reprocessing. So we should not have a visited set that prevents reprocessing. Instead, we keep a set of nodes that are currently in the queue (to avoid duplicate entries). When we pop, we remove it from that set. Then after removal, if we add parent, we check if it's not already in the queue (to avoid duplicates) and add.\n\nWe need to compute children for each node to determine leaf status. We can build a children adjacency list from parent pointers. When we remove a node, we remove it from its parent's children list. Also remove it from the global set of nodes.\n\nWe also need to handle root: root is not removable, but can become leaf? It has no parent. It might be added to queue? Initially root is in queue. But root is not removable, so it will not be removed. But it might be revisited if its children are removed? Possibly.\n\nNow, the termination: when queue is empty and no more nodes to process.\n\nNow, the output: retained_node_ids (list of strings) sorted? The schema just says array of strings, probably the order doesn't matter? But likely the order should be deterministic. The description doesn't specify order, so maybe any order? But to be safe, we can sort them. The visit_order: the complete visit order in which nodes were popped from queue (including skipped? The description: \"Return retained IDs and the complete visit order.\" I think visit_order should list all node IDs in the order they were considered (popped) regardless of removal or not? Or only those that were actually processed? The phrase \"visit order\" suggests the order we visited nodes (i.e., considered them for removal). So we record each pop.\n\nLet's test with first fixture case. We'll simulate manually to verify. But we need to write Python code.\n\nImplementation plan:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # case is dict with \"nodes\" list and \"utility_threshold\" float, maybe \"_case_nonce\" ignored.\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    threshold = case[\"utility_threshold\"]\n\n    # Build mapping from node id to node dict\n    node_map = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    # Build children list for each node (list of child ids)\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        pid = n[\"parent\"]\n        if pid is not None:\n            children[pid].append(n[\"id\"])\n\n    # Set of nodes that are not removed (initially all)\n    present = set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes)\n\n    # Priority queue: we want descending depth. Use heap with (-depth, node_id)\n    import heapq\n    heap = []\n    # To avoid duplicates in heap, maintain a set of enqueued nodes\n    enqueued = set()\n    for n in nodes:\n        heapq.heappush(heap, (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n        enqueued.add(n[\"id\"])\n\n    visit_order = []\n\n    while heap:\n        neg_depth, node_id = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        enqueued.discard(node_id)\n        # Skip if node is already removed\n        if node_id not in present:\n            continue\n        # Record visit\n        visit_order.append(node_id)\n        # Check if node is removable leaf with utility <= threshold\n        node = node_map[node_id]\n        if node[\"removable\"] and node[\"utility\"] <= threshold:\n            # Check leaf: no children that are present\n            leaf = True\n            for child_id in children[node_id]:\n                if child_id in present:\n                    leaf = False\n                    break\n            if leaf:\n                # Remove node\n                present.remove(node_id)\n                # Remove from parent's children list\n                parent_id = node[\"parent\"]\n                if parent_id is not None:\n                    # Remove child from parent's list\n                    children[parent_id].remove(node_id)\n                    # Enqueue parent if not already enqueued and still present\n                    if parent_id in present and parent_id not in enqueued:\n                        heapq.heappush(heap, (-node_map[parent_id][\"depth\"], parent_id))\n                        enqueued.add(parent_id)\n        # else: node not removable or not leaf or utility above threshold, do nothing\n\n    # retained_node_ids: all nodes still present\n    retained = sorted(present)  # sorted for determinism\n    # visit_order already in order of processing\n    return {\"retained_node_ids\": retained, \"visit_order\": visit_order}\n\nBut need to check: The initial queue order: \"Initialize a queue in descending depth and stable node-ID order.\" Using heap with (-depth, node_id) will give descending depth, and for same depth, node_id ascending (since heap sorts by tuple). That matches stable node-ID order (assuming lexicographic). Good.\n\nOne nuance: When we enqueue parent after removal, we push with (-parent_depth, parent_id). This will place it in the correct order relative to other nodes. Since parent depth is lower than child, it will be popped later (since -depth is negative, lower depth gives larger negative? Actually depth 1 gives -1, depth 2 gives -2. -2 < -1, so depth 2 popped first. So parent with depth 1 has -1, which is larger than -2, so it will be popped after depth 2 nodes. Good.\n\nBut we need to ensure that we don't process the same node multiple times if it is re-enqueued and then popped again. That's allowed. The algorithm should handle that.\n\nEdge cases: root has depth 0, not removable, but if it becomes leaf (all children removed), it remains. It might be re-enqueued if a child is removed? Actually if a child of root is removed, we enqueue root. Root might be already in heap? Initially root is in heap. After all initial nodes are popped, root would have been popped early (depth 0, so -0 =0, other depths negative, so depth 0 pops last? Actually -0 = 0, -1 = -1, -2 = -2, so heap order: -2 first, then -1, then 0. So root pops after all others. So when we remove a child of root, we enqueue root (if not already enqueued). But root might have been popped already? Actually root is depth 0, so it pops after all depth 2 and depth 1 nodes. So if we remove a child of root during processing of depth 2 or depth 1, root is still in heap? Yes, because we haven't popped root yet (since depth 0 is last). So we need to avoid adding root again if it's already enqueued. Our check: if parent_id not in enqueued, then push. Since root is already enqueued initially, we won't add again. But what if root has already been popped? It will not be added again because we only enqueue if parent is present and not enqueued. But if root was popped and not removed, it's no longer in enqueued, but we might want to revisit it. However, root is not removable, so even if it becomes leaf, we don't need to do anything. So it's fine. But if a node becomes leaf after its children are removed, we might need to consider it for removal even if it was already visited earlier? For non-root nodes that are removable, if they were visited earlier but not leaf at that time, they become leaf later. But our algorithm processes nodes in descending depth, so a node at depth 2 will be processed before its parent at depth 1. If the depth 2 node is removed, we enqueue the depth 1 parent. That parent may have already been processed (since depth 1 nodes are processed after depth 2? Actually ordering: depth 2 first, then depth 1, then depth 0. So a depth 1 node would be processed after all depth 2 nodes. So if we remove a depth 2 node, we enqueue its depth 1 parent. But the depth 1 nodes have not been processed yet (they are still in heap), so we are effectively adding it to the heap again (if not already). But it's already there. So we might have duplicates. Our duplicate avoidance using enqueued set prevents that. However, it might be that the parent was popped already if we processed another child? Actually depth 1 nodes are processed after depth 2, so they are still in heap. So they are enqueued. So adding again is prevented. But what if the parent was already popped? That can only happen if depth 1 nodes are processed, but depth 2 nodes are still being processed. Since depth 1 nodes have lower depth, they are processed after all depth 2 nodes. So when we process depth 2 nodes, depth 1 nodes are still in heap. So no issue. However, if a depth 1 node has a child at depth 2 that is removed, we enqueue the depth 1 parent, but it's still in heap. So fine.\n\nBut after processing all depth 2 nodes, we move to depth 1 nodes. At that point, if we remove a depth 1 node, we might enqueue its parent (depth 0). But depth 0 root is still in heap (since depth 0 is last). So again prevented. So the algorithm should work.\n\nOne more nuance: After removal of a node, we update children list of parent by removing the child. But we also need to check leaf condition based on children that are present. Since we remove child from children[parent_id], that list reflects present children. Good.\n\nNow, test with first case. Let's simulate mentally