ild n5 present. So not leaf. Also not removable false. So nothing. Visit order: n3\n\nroot: present, children root: n1, n2, n3 (all present). Not leaf. Not removable. Visit order: root.\n\nEnd. retained_node_ids: all except n4 and n6? Wait n5 is present, n1,n2,n3,root. So retained: ['n1','n2','n3','n5','root']? Actually n5 is not removable, so it stays. So set = {root, n1, n2, n3, n5}. Sort: ['n1','n2','n3','n5','root']? Lexicographically: 'n1','n2','n3','n5','root' (since 'r' > 'n'? Actually 'r' > 'n' so root after n5). So order: n1, n2, n3, n5, root. Visit order: n4, n5, n6, n1, n2, n3, root.\n\nWe can check if this matches expected? We don't have expected output, but it seems plausible.\n\nNow, we need to ensure we handle the case where a node becomes leaf after its children are removed, and it is removable and utility <= threshold, even if it was already visited earlier. For example, suppose n2 was initially not a leaf because it had child n4. After removing n4, n2 becomes leaf. But n2 was not visited yet (since it's depth1, we haven't popped it yet). So it will be processed later and we will check leaf condition. But if n2 had been popped earlier? In our order, depth2 is processed before depth1, so n2 is not popped until after all depth2 are processed. So it's fine. However, consider a scenario where a node is removable and initially leaf but utility > threshold, so it's not removed. Later, after some removals, its utility might change? No, utility is fixed. So it will never become removable. So no need to revisit.\n\nAnother scenario: a node that is not removable but becomes leaf after its children are removed. That doesn't trigger removal. But it might be revisited? Not needed.\n\nThus the algorithm seems correct.\n\nOne more nuance: The parent of a removed node might be the same as another removed node? Not possible.\n\nNow, we must write Python code that exactly matches the output schema: returning dict with \"retained_node_ids\" (list of strings) and \"vis